// Permit a Read only if the same user logged in within the last hour and has not
// logged out since.
//
// `since` says both halves at once: the right operand, a successful Login, is the
// anchor that must have happened within the hour, and the left operand must have
// held at every step from that login to the read being decided. The left is a
// negation, so a Logout at any step after the login ends the permit; a new Login
// after it starts a new one. Writing the negation as a second conjunct instead,
// `formerly … && !Logout…`, would test only the occurrence being decided, which
// is a Read, so a logout would never reach it.
//
// The event schema beside this file pins `callerPrincipal`, so every temporal
// predicate is automatically correlated to the principal of the request being
// decided: another user's Login is invisible to this rule, and there is nothing
// in the policy that has to say so.
@id("read_after_login")
permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    !Drupe::Action::"Logout"::response{ input.user: context.input.user }
    since within 1h
    Drupe::Action::"Login"::response{ input.user: context.input.user }
};
